            <div xml:id="d549">

                <pb facs="0645" n="573" sameAs="frus:frus1909/pg573"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 10044/172.</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Ambassador Leishman to the Secretary of
                    State.</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Telegram.—Paraphrase.]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >American Embassy</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Pera</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1909-05-01"><hi
                                rendition="#italic">May 1, 1909</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>Mr. Leishman says as conditions in disturbed districts have improved greatly and
                    as the Government is taking very energetic measures to suppress further attempt
                    at disorder and punish perpetrators of the recent trouble, he suggests holding
                        <hi rendition="#italic">North Carolina</hi> and <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Montana</hi> at Gibraltar, or some other Mediterranean port, for further
                    orders, instead of sending them to Turkish waters. He says the need for their
                    immediate presence no longer exists, and that if conditions continue to improve,
                    as is probable, their visit might be regarded as unnecessarily offensive to the
                    new Government, which appears to be both desirous and able to restore peace and
                    quiet.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
